63.3 ms. 45% of it in Seq Scan on orders, which reads 200,000 rows to keep 1,000.

Planning 0.312 ms · Execution 63.3 ms · Buffers 4,834 pages, 100% from cache

Share     Time              Node                                       Rows     Estimate  Buffers
  45%  28.5 ms  ████▌       Seq Scan on orders                        1,000  66,667 ▼67×    2,417
  34%  21.8 ms  ███▌        └─ [InitPlan 1 (returns $0)] Aggregate        1            1        0
  20%  12.9 ms  ██             └─ Seq Scan on orders orders_1       200,000      200,000    2,417

Findings (2)

  MEDIUM  ES001 Selective sequential scan
          Seq Scan on orders reads 200,000 rows to keep 1,000.
          Rows kept: 1,000 of 200,000 (0.50%)
          Filter: (orders.amount > $0)
          Table size: 4,834 pages (37.8 MB)
          Time in the node: 28.5 ms (45% of the runtime)
          → An index on orders (amount) would let PostgreSQL read only the matching rows.

  LOW     ES002 Row misestimate
          Seq Scan on orders returned 1,000 rows where the planner expected 66,667: 67× fewer.
          Estimated rows: 66,667
          Actual rows: 1,000
          → The condition compares with $0, a value known only when the query runs, so the planner
          used a default guess. ANALYZE cannot change that; it matters only if a plan choice above
          depends on this estimate.

Advice (1)

  LIKELY  Index orders (amount)
          CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ON public.orders (amount);
          An index on orders (amount) would let Seq Scan on orders read only the rows it keeps
          instead of the whole table.
          ! Not connected to the database: existing indexes, the table's write load and the column
          statistics were not checked.
          ! The condition compares with a value known only at run time, so whether the planner uses
          the index depends on that value.

No index would help
  Seq Scan on orders orders_1: it reads the whole table because the query needs every row; there is
  no condition for an index to serve.

Share and Time: time spent in each node itself, wall-clock. Rows: actual rows per loop, ×loops when
it ran more than once. ▲/▼: actual rows above or below the estimate by 10× or more. Buffers: pages
each node read itself.
